1
00:00:01,220 --> 00:00:05,390
Imagine that you're starting a new
project with a brand new team of people.

2
00:00:05,390 --> 00:00:07,702
No one on the team has
worked together before,

3
00:00:07,702 --> 00:00:10,030
so this will be a new experience for
everyone.

4
00:00:11,060 --> 00:00:14,363
To better understand how your
team might develop over time,

5
00:00:14,363 --> 00:00:19,870
let's discuss the psychologist Bruce
Tuckman's five stages of team development.

6
00:00:19,870 --> 00:00:25,186
Forming, storming, norming,
performing and adjourning.

7
00:00:25,186 --> 00:00:29,397
These stages of development illustrate
how teams grow from a disparate group

8
00:00:29,397 --> 00:00:31,610
of people into a high functioning unit.

9
00:00:32,650 --> 00:00:35,607
You might even recognize
the characteristics of each stage from

10
00:00:35,607 --> 00:00:38,240
previous experiences working on a team.

11
00:00:38,240 --> 00:00:42,370
Tuchman's first stage of team
development is the forming stage.

12
00:00:42,370 --> 00:00:44,940
At this point everything feels shiny and
new.

13
00:00:44,940 --> 00:00:47,515
Individuals on the team are just
getting to know on another, and

14
00:00:47,515 --> 00:00:49,240
they're eager to make a good impression.

15
00:00:49,240 --> 00:00:53,020
And typically they're excited for
the work to begin.

16
00:00:53,020 --> 00:00:57,330
During this stage, you as a project
manager should clarify project goals,

17
00:00:57,330 --> 00:01:00,270
roles, and context about the project.

18
00:01:00,270 --> 00:01:04,121
People are seeking guidance and
it's your job to provide that guidance.

19
00:01:04,121 --> 00:01:07,336
The second stage of team
development is the storming stage,

20
00:01:07,336 --> 00:01:10,100
this is where things get a bit trickier.

21
00:01:10,100 --> 00:01:13,884
As people settle into their roles and
the work on their project begins,

22
00:01:13,884 --> 00:01:17,809
the people on your team are interacting
more and maybe disagreeing a bit.

23
00:01:17,809 --> 00:01:21,320
This is where feelings of
frustration might emerge.

24
00:01:21,320 --> 00:01:25,611
Individuals might take issues with certain
processes they feel are inefficient, or

25
00:01:25,611 --> 00:01:29,484
other teammates they disagree with,
especially when the team is navigating

26
00:01:29,484 --> 00:01:32,480
tasks that are much more complex
than they first appeared.

27
00:01:32,480 --> 00:01:33,770
It makes sense, right?

28
00:01:33,770 --> 00:01:37,068
If you're working closely with a new
group of people for the first time,

29
00:01:37,068 --> 00:01:40,140
there's bound to be some
interpersonal conflict.

30
00:01:40,140 --> 00:01:42,295
Teammates might disagree on time, and

31
00:01:42,295 --> 00:01:45,691
effort estimations, vary in their
levels of independence, or

32
00:01:45,691 --> 00:01:50,160
prefer to prioritize certain tasks
over other equally important tasks.

33
00:01:50,160 --> 00:01:53,960
As the project manager, it's your
job to focus on conflict resolution.

34
00:01:53,960 --> 00:01:56,471
Listen as the team addresses
problems to solve and

35
00:01:56,471 --> 00:01:59,650
share insights on how the team
might better function as a unit.

36
00:02:00,870 --> 00:02:05,280
After storming comes Tuchman's
third stage, the norming stage.

37
00:02:05,280 --> 00:02:06,082
At this point,

38
00:02:06,082 --> 00:02:10,229
the team has resolved some of its internal
conflict by establishing new norms.

39
00:02:10,229 --> 00:02:15,410
Like processes and workflows that make it
easier for everyone to get things done.

40
00:02:15,410 --> 00:02:19,730
The team feels better equipped to work
together efficiently and effectively.

41
00:02:19,730 --> 00:02:22,674
You, as the project manager
should codify the team norms,

42
00:02:22,674 --> 00:02:27,560
ensuring that the team is aware of those
norms and reinforce them when needed.

43
00:02:27,560 --> 00:02:32,137
For example, if you've agreed to discuss
solutions to issues during weekly team

44
00:02:32,137 --> 00:02:37,240
meetings, ensure that your weekly agenda
budgets time for this topic each week.

45
00:02:37,240 --> 00:02:40,570
Tuchman's fourth stage
is the performing stage.

46
00:02:40,570 --> 00:02:44,863
During this time, the team works together
relatively seamlessly to complete tasks,

47
00:02:44,863 --> 00:02:48,900
reach milestones and
make progress toward the project goal.

48
00:02:48,900 --> 00:02:52,015
In the performing stage,
you as the project manager should focus on

49
00:02:52,015 --> 00:02:56,590
delegating, motivating and providing
feedback to keep up the team's momentum.

50
00:02:56,590 --> 00:03:00,960
The fifth and final stage of team
development is the adjourning stage.

51
00:03:00,960 --> 00:03:05,690
In this stage the project is wrapping up
and it's time for the team to disband.

52
00:03:05,690 --> 00:03:07,622
It can be a bittersweet time for
the team and

53
00:03:07,622 --> 00:03:10,530
you might want to mark the end of
the project with a celebration.

54
00:03:11,620 --> 00:03:15,588
You as the project manager should set up
time to celebrate the final milestones and

55
00:03:15,588 --> 00:03:17,267
success of the project as a group.

56
00:03:17,267 --> 00:03:21,310
And be sure each member of your
team knows what's next for them.

57
00:03:21,310 --> 00:03:25,664
Okay, to recap, Tuckman's five stages
of team development are forming,

58
00:03:25,664 --> 00:03:28,667
storming, norming,
performing and adjourning.

59
00:03:28,667 --> 00:03:31,758
These stages of team
development come together to

60
00:03:31,758 --> 00:03:36,690
form a helpful framework you can use to
assess and recognize team dynamics, and

61
00:03:36,690 --> 00:03:39,791
then adjust your management
style accordingly.

62
00:03:39,791 --> 00:03:43,921
Team dynamics refer to the forces
both conscious and unconscious,

63
00:03:43,921 --> 00:03:46,680
that impact team behavior and performance.

64
00:03:47,830 --> 00:03:52,320
Managing team dynamics is a big part of
determining how to motivate your team.

65
00:03:52,320 --> 00:03:57,060
It might be tempting to assume that people
can go full-speed ahead into a project.

66
00:03:57,060 --> 00:04:00,222
But in reality,
it's important to take time to understand

67
00:04:00,222 --> 00:04:05,470
the dynamics of your team overall and how
individual team members are fitting in.

68
00:04:05,470 --> 00:04:09,015
This is especially important during
the more precarious forming and

69
00:04:09,015 --> 00:04:10,000
storming stages.

70
00:04:11,130 --> 00:04:14,660
Let's break down why managing
team dynamics is so important.

71
00:04:14,660 --> 00:04:17,801
First, teams have individuals
with different skill sets,

72
00:04:17,801 --> 00:04:20,889
varying degrees of autonomy,
and competing priorities.

73
00:04:21,960 --> 00:04:25,848
It's your job to forge consensus and
set clear purposes, goals,

74
00:04:25,848 --> 00:04:28,980
dependencies and accountability.

75
00:04:28,980 --> 00:04:33,950
When teams function cohesively, they can
focus on the tasks and objectives at hand.

76
00:04:33,950 --> 00:04:37,646
Another reason why it's important to
manage team dynamics is that it helps you

77
00:04:37,646 --> 00:04:41,680
to create a collaborative and
psychologically safe environment.

78
00:04:41,680 --> 00:04:45,228
When team members feel safe,
they're willing to help one another, and

79
00:04:45,228 --> 00:04:47,640
accept help when they need it.

80
00:04:47,640 --> 00:04:51,440
This benefits the entire project
by keeping schedules on track.

81
00:04:51,440 --> 00:04:54,234
Though it can take time to
get to the performing stage,

82
00:04:54,234 --> 00:04:58,064
using the norming stage as a time to
foster a collaborative environment can

83
00:04:58,064 --> 00:04:59,550
help you get there quicker.

84
00:05:01,030 --> 00:05:01,929
Understanding and

85
00:05:01,929 --> 00:05:06,630
managing team dynamics can also help you
understand how to motivate your team.

86
00:05:06,630 --> 00:05:10,711
Motivated team members are likely to
contribute more to discussions, complete

87
00:05:10,711 --> 00:05:14,980
their tasks and actively participate
in other project activities.

88
00:05:14,980 --> 00:05:18,700
A positive team atmosphere can
help employees feel empowered.

89
00:05:18,700 --> 00:05:21,093
More comfortable taking calculated risks,
and

90
00:05:21,093 --> 00:05:25,100
more likely to seek out innovative
solutions to complex problems.

91
00:05:25,100 --> 00:05:29,040
Remember, so much of team dynamics
happens under the surface.

92
00:05:29,040 --> 00:05:33,047
Identifying and understanding the stages
of team development can help you make

93
00:05:33,047 --> 00:05:35,630
sense of how dynamics
are playing out on your team.

94
00:05:36,680 --> 00:05:38,699
This can help you to be a better leader.

95
00:05:39,750 --> 00:05:42,629
Coming up, we'll discuss what
it means to promote ethical and

96
00:05:42,629 --> 00:05:45,070
inclusive leadership in the workplace.

97
00:05:45,070 --> 00:05:45,570
Meet you there.
